        <div id="part-number" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How to Find Your Refrigerator Water Filter: Match the Part Number First
          </h2>

          <p>
            The best refrigerator water filter is the one that fits your fridge, and the only reliable way to know is the part number. Fridge brands use different filter shapes, and even one brand can use five or six cartridges across its range. Buy by part number, not by fridge brand or by a photo that looks right.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Read the Label on the Old Cartridge</h3>

          <p>
            The fastest route is the filter you already have. Pull it out and read the label: it will say something like EDR1RXD1 or Filter 1, HAF-QIN, LT1000P, RPWFE or EPTWFU01. Take a photo of it before you throw it away. That number is what you search for, and it is what every replacement listing cross-references.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">No Filter in the Fridge? Use the Model Number</h3>

          <p>
            If the old filter is missing or the label has worn off, find the refrigerator's model number on the sticker inside the fresh-food compartment, usually on a side wall or the ceiling. The owner's manual or the maker's parts page will list the correct filter for that model. Third-party listings also print long lists of compatible fridge model numbers, which helps as a cross-check.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Watch for Look-Alike Numbers</h3>

          <p>
            Most wrong orders come from near-identical numbers. Samsung's square HAF-QIN (DA97-17376B) is not the round HAF-CIN (DA29-00020B). GE's RPWFE is not the older MWF or XWF. Whirlpool's everydrop Filter 1 is a completely different cartridge from Filter 4. Waterdrop even warns that its Bosch replacement fits BORPLFTR10, not BORPLFTR20. One digit decides whether the filter locks in.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="nsf-certification" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. NSF 42, 53 and 401: What a Fridge Filter Certification Actually Means
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every refrigerator filter on Amazon says it is certified, but the certification number tells you what it actually does. Three NSF/ANSI standards matter for drinking water, and a fourth is often listed to make a cheap filter look better qualified than it is.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">NSF/ANSI 42: Taste and Odor</h3>

          <p>
            Standard 42 covers aesthetic effects: chlorine taste and odor, and particulates. It is the baseline, and it is all most cheap replacement filters carry. A filter certified only to 42 will make tap water taste better, but it makes no certified claim about lead or any health-related contaminant.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">NSF/ANSI 53: Health Contaminants Such as Lead</h3>

          <p>
            Standard 53 covers health effects, such as lead, cysts, mercury, some volatile organic compounds and, for filters certified for it, PFOA and PFOS. If you live in an older house or a city with lead service lines, 53 is the number to look for. The genuine everydrop, GE and LG filters in this guide list it, as do the Waterdrop Plus replacements.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">NSF/ANSI 401: Emerging Contaminants</h3>

          <p>
            Standard 401 covers incidental contaminants that are increasingly found at low levels in tap water, such as some pharmaceuticals, BPA and certain pesticides. Waterdrop's Samsung replacement lists 99.2 percent reduction of BPA, ibuprofen, naproxen, estrone and phenytoin under 401.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">NSF/ANSI 372: Lead-Free Materials, Not Lead Removal</h3>

          <p>
            Standard 372 means the filter's own materials are low in lead. It says nothing about removing lead from your water. Many generics list NSF 42 and 372 side by side, which reads well but adds up to taste and odor only. You can check any claim by searching the brand and model on NSF's public listings.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="oem-vs-generic" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. OEM vs Third-Party Fridge Filters: Generic Is Fine Only With the Right Certification
          </h2>

          <p>
            Genuine filters cost more than generics, and the price gap is real enough that most owners ask whether it is worth paying. The honest answer depends on what the generic is certified for, not on the brand name.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Genuine (OEM) Filters</h3>

          <p>
            A genuine filter from the fridge maker, such as everydrop for Whirlpool or GE's RPWFE, is the part the refrigerator was designed around. Fit and seal are guaranteed, warranty questions do not arise, and the leading genuine filters publish NSF 42, 53 and 401 coverage. Samsung adds an authentication tag so you can spot counterfeits, which are common for popular part numbers.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Certified Third-Party Filters</h3>

          <p>
            A small group of third-party filters carry the same NSF 53 and 401 certification as the genuine part. Waterdrop's Plus line is the clearest example in this guide. For an out-of-warranty fridge, a certified third-party filter is a reasonable way to save money without giving up filtration.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Uncertified and 42-Only Generics</h3>

          <p>
            Most cheap replacement filters list NSF 42 and 372, or a lab test from an unnamed laboratory. They improve taste, and many owners are happy with them, but they do not carry a certified claim for lead. If lead or PFAS is the reason you filter, skip them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Chips, Reset Lights and Fit</h3>

          <p>
            Some refrigerators check for a chip in the cartridge. Third-party RPWFE replacements advertise a built-in chip for exactly this reason. Other fridges simply count time or gallons and need the reminder light reset by hand. Either way, a generic that fits loosely can leak, so buy from a brand that lists your exact part number. The table below shows every pick at a glance.
          </p>

