          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Which Water Filter Fits My Refrigerator? A Brand-by-Brand Guide
          </h2>

          <p>
            Use this as a starting point, then confirm against the number on your cartridge. Fridge makers change filters between model years, so two refrigerators of the same brand and age can still take different parts.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Whirlpool, Maytag, KitchenAid, Amana and JennAir</h3>

          <p>
            These brands share everydrop filters, numbered 1 to 5 and beyond. Filter 1 (EDR1RXD1) is common in side-by-side and French-door models with the filter inside the top of the fresh-food compartment. Filter 4 (EDR4RXD1, also UKF8001) is common in bottom-freezer and French-door models. Kenmore models built by Whirlpool use the same cartridges under Kenmore numbers such as 9081 and 9006.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Samsung</h3>

          <p>
            Samsung's main current filter is the square HAF-QIN (DA97-17376B). Older and some current models use the round HAF-CIN (DA29-00020B), and some use the HAF-HIN family. Check the shape of your old filter and the label before ordering.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">LG and LG-Built Kenmore</h3>

          <p>
            LG's LT1000P covers many French-door models; the listing names ADQ74793501 and ADQ75795105 as the same filter, and third-party listings cross-reference Kenmore 46-9980. Other LG fridges use LT700P, LT800P or LT1000PC, so match the number.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">GE, GE Profile, Cafe and Monogram</h3>

          <p>
            RPWFE is the filter for many GE French-door models and has a chip the fridge reads. Side-by-side and older models often use MWF or XWF instead, which are a different shape entirely.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Frigidaire and Electrolux</h3>

          <p>
            Frigidaire's PureSource Ultra II (EPTWFU01) is the current mainstream filter. Older models use PureSource 2 or PureSource 3, and Electrolux-branded fridges have their own PureAdvantage cartridges. If you are shopping for the fridge itself, our <a href="/best-refrigerator-brands/">refrigerator brands guide</a> covers which makers are worth buying.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-often" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Often to Change a Refrigerator Water Filter: Every Six Months, or Sooner
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every filter in this guide is rated for six months. LG's listing adds a 200-gallon limit, whichever comes first. Those numbers are not marketing: a carbon block has a finite capacity, and once it is saturated it stops reducing contaminants even though water still flows through it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Six-Month, 200-Gallon Rule</h3>

          <p>
            A household that drinks a lot of fridge water, fills pitchers and uses the ice maker heavily can pass 200 gallons well before six months. A single person might not come close. Change on whichever limit you hit first, and if your water is high in sediment, expect to change sooner because the filter clogs.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Signs the Filter Is Done</h3>

          <p>
            Slower dispenser flow, a chlorine taste returning, cloudy or bad-tasting ice and a reminder light are all signs. Do not wait for flow to stop. A filter certified to reduce lead only does that within its rated capacity, so running one past its date gives you a false sense of protection.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Resetting the Reminder Light</h3>

          <p>
            Most fridges do not detect a new filter; they count time. On Frigidaire models you hold the Water Filter button for three seconds. GE third-party listings say to hold the Water button for three seconds if the light stays on. Check your manual for the exact combination, and write the change date on the cartridge so you are not relying on the light alone. The same logic applies to pitchers; our guide on <a href="/how-often-to-change-brita-filter/">how often to change a Brita filter</a> covers it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="replace" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How to Replace a Refrigerator Water Filter in Five Minutes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Replacing a fridge filter needs no tools and, on almost every current model, no water shutoff. The whole job takes a few minutes, most of it spent flushing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Find the Housing</h3>

          <p>
            Filters live in one of three places: inside the fresh-food compartment at the top corner, in the base grille below the doors, or behind a panel in the door. Your old cartridge's position tells you the style.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Remove the Old Filter</h3>

          <p>
            Keep a towel underneath, because a little water always escapes. Most filters turn a quarter turn counterclockwise and pull straight out; Frigidaire's EPTWFU01 turns 90 degrees counterclockwise. Some base-grille filters have a push button that ejects the cartridge.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Install the New Filter</h3>

          <p>
            Remove the cap, line up the arrows or grip as the instructions show, push firmly and turn until it clicks or stops. A filter that does not seat fully is the most common cause of leaks and of no water at the dispenser.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Flush It</h3>

          <p>
            New carbon filters release fine carbon dust and trapped air. Several replacement listings say to run about four gallons through the dispenser before drinking. Expect sputtering at first and some grey specks; both clear as you flush.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 5: Reset the Light and Check for Drips</h3>

          <p>
            Reset the reminder, then check the housing for drips after an hour. Discard the first batch of ice, because it was made with unfiltered or partly flushed water.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-it-removes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Does a Refrigerator Water Filter Remove Lead, PFAS and Microplastics? Some Do
          </h2>

          <p>
            A refrigerator filter is a compact carbon block, so what it removes depends entirely on how it is certified. The best ones are capable point-of-use filters; the cheapest only improve taste.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Chlorine Taste and Odor</h3>

          <p>
            Every filter here reduces chlorine taste and odor. If your only complaint is that tap water tastes like a swimming pool, even an NSF 42-only generic will fix it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Lead</h3>

          <p>
            Lead reduction requires NSF 53 certification for lead. LG lists almost all lead removed, GE lists lead among its 70-plus impurities, and Frigidaire lists lead. If lead is your concern, confirm the specific claim on NSF's listings rather than trusting the front of the box.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">PFAS (PFOA and PFOS)</h3>

          <p>
            GE's RPWFE names PFOA and PFOS in its certified impurities. Waterdrop lists PFAS reduction for its Plus filters, with the Samsung version citing a third-party lab figure. If PFAS has been found in your water, a certified filter is the minimum; an under-sink system is the stronger fix.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Microplastics</h3>

          <p>
            everydrop lists 99 percent microplastic reduction for Filter 1, and GE lists microplastics for RPWFE. Most generics do not mention them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What a Fridge Filter Will Not Do</h3>

          <p>
            A carbon fridge filter is not rated to disinfect well water, soften hard water, or remove dissolved minerals and salts. Hard water will still leave scale in your ice maker. For those problems you need different equipment, starting with our guide to the <a href="/best-water-filtration-system-for-home/">best water filtration systems for home</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-pitcher-under-sink" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Refrigerator Filter vs Pitcher vs Under-Sink: Which Is Better?
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you already own a fridge with a dispenser, its filter is the most convenient filtered water in the house. Whether it is the best depends on what you need to remove and how much water you use.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Refrigerator Filter</h3>

          <p>
            Cold filtered water and ice on tap, with a six-month change and no counter space. Flow is limited by the dispenser, and capacity is roughly 200 gallons per cartridge. It suits households whose main concern is taste and who want filtered ice.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Water Filter Pitcher</h3>

          <p>
            A pitcher works in any kitchen and many are certified for lead and PFAS, but you refill it constantly and cartridges last a couple of months. It is a good choice if your fridge has no dispenser; see our <a href="/best-water-filter-pitcher/">best water filter pitchers</a> for certified options.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Under-Sink Filter</h3>

          <p>
            An under-sink carbon or reverse osmosis system filters far more water per cartridge, flows at faucet speed, and RO models reduce dissolved solids that no fridge filter touches. Many can also feed the fridge line, so the fridge gets the same water. Our <a href="/best-under-sink-water-filter/">best under-sink water filters</a> guide covers the options.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Whole-House Filtration</h3>

          <p>
            If the problem is sediment, iron or well water, treat it where the water enters the house so the fridge filter is not doing a job it was never designed for. A whole-house sediment filter also makes every fridge cartridge last longer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="problems" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Fridge Filter Problems: Slow Flow, Leaks and Bad Taste
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most problems after a filter change are installation problems, not faulty cartridges. Work through these before you send a filter back.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Slow Dispenser or No Water</h3>

          <p>
            A filter that is not fully seated blocks flow. Remove it and reinstall it, turning until it stops. Air trapped in a new filter also slows the dispenser for the first few gallons. If flow was slow before the change, the line or the inlet valve may be the cause, not the filter.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Leaking Around the Housing</h3>

          <p>
            Check that the O-rings on the new cartridge are seated and undamaged, and that you removed any protective cap. Leaks are where cheap uncertified generics most often fail, which is why Samsung and LG both warn about third-party fit.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Black Specks or Cloudy Water</h3>

          <p>
            Fine carbon particles and dissolved air are normal for the first few gallons. Keep flushing. Cloudiness from air clears from the bottom of the glass up within a minute.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Bad Taste After a Change</h3>

          <p>
            A plastic or carbon taste usually means the filter needs more flushing. If water tastes worse than before after several gallons, the cartridge may be counterfeit or the wrong part; check the label against your old one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying by Fridge Brand Instead of Part Number</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Samsung, Whirlpool, LG and GE each use several different filters. Searching for a Samsung fridge filter and buying the first result is how people end up with a round HAF-CIN for a fridge that takes the square HAF-QIN. Read the number on your old cartridge and buy that number.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting NSF 372 as a Lead Claim</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                NSF 372 means the filter's own parts are low in lead; it does not mean the filter removes lead from water. A generic listing NSF 42 and 372 is certified for taste and odor only. For lead, look for NSF 53 with lead named, and check it on NSF's public listings.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running the Filter Past Six Months</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A saturated carbon block keeps passing water long after it stops reducing contaminants, so flow is not a sign it still works. Change at six months or the rated gallons, whichever comes first, and write the date on the cartridge rather than relying on the reminder light.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Flush After Installing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A new filter releases carbon fines and trapped air. Drinking the first glass gives you grey specks and a flat taste, and the first ice batch is made with partly flushed water. Run a few gallons through the dispenser and discard the first ice.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Suspiciously Cheap Genuine Filters</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Counterfeit filters for popular part numbers are common, and a fake can leak or filter poorly. Buy genuine filters from the brand's own storefront or a reputable seller, and use Samsung's authentication tag where it is provided.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best refrigerator water filter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The best refrigerator water filter is the genuine filter for your fridge's part number, or a third-party replacement with the same NSF 42, 53 and 401 certification. For Whirlpool-family fridges, everydrop Filter 1 is our top pick; GE owners should buy RPWFE, LG owners LT1000P. Match the part number on your old cartridge before comparing anything else.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are off-brand refrigerator water filters safe?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Certified ones are. A third-party filter certified to NSF 53 and 401, such as Waterdrop's Plus line, offers the same certified reduction as many genuine filters. Uncertified or NSF 42-only generics are safe to drink through but only improve taste, and poorly made ones can leak. Check the exact certification and the part number before you buy.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know which water filter my refrigerator uses?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Read the part number on your current filter, such as EDR1RXD1, HAF-QIN, LT1000P or RPWFE. If the filter is missing, find the model number on the sticker inside the fresh-food compartment and look it up in the owner's manual or the maker's parts site. Replacement listings also print the fridge models each filter fits.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do refrigerator water filters remove lead?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only filters certified to NSF/ANSI 53 for lead are proven to reduce it. Genuine filters from LG, GE and Whirlpool's everydrop list lead reduction, and the Waterdrop Plus replacements list NSF 53. Many cheap generics are certified only to NSF 42 for taste and odor, which makes no claim about lead in the water.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What happens if you don't change your refrigerator water filter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The carbon block saturates and stops reducing contaminants, flow slows as sediment builds up, and the water and ice can start to taste stale. An overdue filter does not usually make water dangerous, but it no longer does what it is certified to do. Change it every six months or at its rated gallons.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use my refrigerator without a water filter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many fridges work without a filter if you install the bypass plug that came with the refrigerator, or one sold for your model. Without a plug, some models will not dispense water at all. Running unfiltered also means sediment reaches the ice maker and valve, so use a bypass only as a temporary measure.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is refrigerator filtered water as good as bottled water?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A refrigerator filter certified to NSF 42, 53 and 401 gives you water that tastes clean and has reduced levels of chlorine, lead and many other contaminants, at a fraction of the cost of bottled water. It does not remove dissolved minerals the way reverse osmosis does. For most city water, a certified fridge filter is entirely adequate.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

